## Unit Files as Attributes

Instead of `content` or `source`, the unit file can be written as attributes:

- Every section is an attribute named after it: `unit` for `[Unit]`, `install` for `[Install]`, and `service`, `socket`, `mount`, `automount`, `swap`, `timer`, `path_section` and `slice` for the section of the unit's type. (`[Path]` is `path_section`, because `path` is the unit file's path.) A section of another type than the unit's is an error at plan time. Target and device units have no section of their own.
- Every directive that systemd knows in that section is an attribute named in snake case: `ExecStart=` is `exec_start`, `CPUSchedulingPolicy=` is `cpu_scheduling_policy`, `IPAddressAllow=` is `ip_address_allow`. The attributes cover every directive of systemd 239 to 262, including the directives shared through [systemd.exec(5)](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.exec.html), [systemd.kill(5)](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.kill.html) and [systemd.resource-control(5)](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.resource-control.html), and the directives that newer versions ignore. Each attribute links to its documentation, and notes the systemd version that introduced or retired it.
- Directives that systemd accepts several times, each adding to the previous ones (`ExecStart=`, `After=`, `Environment=`, `ListenStream=`, `OnCalendar=`, conditions and so on), are lists of strings, written as one line per element in list order. All other directives are strings; a later assignment would replace an earlier one. Booleans and numbers can be written as such, Terraform converts them to `"true"` and `"5"`, which systemd accepts.
- An empty string writes an empty assignment such as `ExecStart=`, which resets the directive to its default or empties its list. That matters mainly in drop-ins, and in templates whose instances have drop-ins.
- Values are written as they are; unit file specifiers such as `%i` and `%n` and environment variables keep their meaning, and quoting follows the rules of the directive. A value must not contain a line break, start or end with whitespace (which systemd strips), or end with an odd number of backslashes (which would continue the line).
- `extra` in each section takes directives that have no attribute, such as `X-` directives or those of a systemd release newer than the provider; directives that have an attribute are refused there. `extra_sections` takes further sections, such as custom `X-` sections, which systemd ignores.

The provider writes the file in a fixed form: sections in the order `[Unit]`, the type's section, `[Install]`, then `extra_sections` sorted by name; within a section the directives in the order of systemd's own table, then `extra` sorted by name. `content` reports that file, so the plan shows a diff of the file in addition to the changed attributes, and it is known at plan time unless a value is unknown.

A directive that the host's systemd does not know yet is ignored by systemd with a warning in the journal; the unit still loads. Values are not validated by the provider, only by systemd: a unit with a fatal error, such as a service without `ExecStart=`, fails the apply when it does not load (see below), and systemd logs and ignores invalid values of other directives.

## Behavior

### Create

1. The provider refuses to create the unit if `/etc/systemd/system/<name>` already exists (import it instead), or if systemd already knows a unit of that name from anywhere else, such as a package's unit in `/usr/lib/systemd/system`, a runtime unit in `/run/systemd/system` or a template whose instance the name is. A file in `/etc/systemd/system` would silently override that unit, and destroying the resource would then stop and disable it. Device, mount and swap units that systemd made for the kernel's devices, mounts and swap areas, and slices, which systemd creates on demand, have no unit file; a unit file for them adds settings and is allowed.
2. The unit file is written atomically with mode `0644`, owned by the user running Terraform.
3. `systemctl daemon-reload` runs, and the provider checks that systemd could load the unit (`LoadState=loaded`). A unit with a fatal error, such as a service without `ExecStart=`, fails the apply. A template is checked through the instance `<name>@sysutils-load-check`, which systemd loads only for the check.
4. If `enabled` is set, the unit is enabled or disabled. If `state` is set, it is started or stopped. Both are checked afterwards: enabling a unit without an `[Install]` section, or starting a unit that exits right away, is an error rather than a permanent diff.

If a step after writing the file fails, the resource is still recorded in state, marked as tainted, so the next apply replaces it and destroy cleans it up.

### Update

When the unit file content differs from the configuration, the file is replaced atomically and `systemctl daemon-reload` runs. Then, if systemd reads the new file differently from the old one (changes to comments, blank lines, whitespace or line continuations only reload):

- If the unit is enabled, it is re-enabled (`systemctl reenable`) so that changes to its `[Install]` section take effect, unless `enabled = false`.
- If the unit is running and `restart_on_change` is `true`, it is restarted with `systemctl try-restart`, unless `state = "stopped"`. For a template, each running instance that uses the template's file is restarted.

After that, `enabled` and `state` are applied as on create. Changing only `enabled`, `state`, `restart_on_change` or `timeout` does not touch the unit file and does not reload systemd.

### Destroy

The unit is stopped (or its failed state reset), disabled, and its file is removed, followed by `systemctl daemon-reload`. For a template, every loaded instance that uses the template's file is stopped. Device units are never stopped, and neither are device, mount, swap and slice units that were already active without a unit file when the resource created its file, such as a file system mounted by hand: removing the file only removes the settings it added. If the file was already deleted out of band, the unit is only stopped if systemd still has it loaded from that file. If something other than a regular file now sits at the unit's path, destroy leaves everything in place and emits a warning.

## Drift Detection

On refresh the provider reads the unit file and runs `systemctl is-enabled` and `systemctl is-active`:

- A changed unit file shows up as a diff of `content`, and of the section attributes for every directive that changed, also when the unit is configured with `content` or `source`. The next apply rewrites the file, reloads systemd and restarts the unit as described above.
- `enabled` is `true` only when `systemctl is-enabled` reports `enabled`. `enabled-runtime`, `static`, `indirect`, `masked` and the like count as `false`.
- `state` is null for templates, which cannot run themselves. Otherwise it is `"running"` when `systemctl is-active` reports `active`, `reloading` or `refreshing`, and `"stopped"` otherwise, including `failed` and `activating`. `systemctl start` waits until the unit has finished starting, so a unit still `activating` afterwards is typically stuck in a restart loop.
- With `enabled` or `state` unset in the configuration, the current value is recorded in state but never changed, and never shows up as a diff. When the unit file changes, they are shown as `(known after apply)`, because re-enabling or restarting the unit can change them.
- If the unit file was deleted, the resource is removed from state and the next plan recreates it.

## Timeouts

Every `systemctl` invocation is limited by `timeout` (2 minutes by default) and killed when it expires, which fails the apply. `systemctl start`, `stop` and `try-restart` wait for the unit's start or stop job, so choose a `timeout` longer than the unit's own `TimeoutStartSec=`/`TimeoutStopSec=`. A job that systemd has already accepted keeps running after `systemctl` is killed.

## Import

An existing unit file in `/etc/systemd/system` can be imported by unit name:

{{ codefile "shell" .ImportFile }}

After import, `content` holds the file's contents, the section attributes the file as systemd reads it, and `enabled` and `state` reflect the unit's current status. `restart_on_change` and `timeout` are set to their defaults. To move an imported unit, or one configured with `content`, to attributes, copy the section attributes from `terraform state show`; the next apply rewrites the file in the provider's form, which only reloads systemd if nothing else changed.

## Caveats

- The resource requires root and a host running systemd as PID 1. It manages the system instance only, not user units (`systemctl --user`).
- Templates (`foo@.service`) cannot be started, so `state` must be unset for them; enable and start instances such as `foo@bar.service` with [`sysutils_service`](./service.md). `enabled = true` enables the instance named by `DefaultInstance=` and fails without it. A unit file for a single instance (`foo@bar.service`) takes precedence over its template and is managed like any other unit, but is refused while the template it is an instance of exists, because the file would override the template for that instance.
- Scope units are created by programs at runtime and have no unit files; configure them with [`sysutils_systemd_dropin`](./systemd_dropin.md).
- The unit name of a mount, automount or swap unit must be its escaped path, such as `srv-data.mount` for `/srv/data` (see `systemd-escape --path`), and that of a device unit the escaped device path. systemd refuses to load a mount unit whose `Where=` does not match its name.
- A `Type=oneshot` service without `RemainAfterExit=yes` becomes inactive as soon as it has run, so `state = "running"` fails for it. Leave `state` unset for such services and start them through a timer or another unit.
- The unit file content is stored in Terraform state, as `content` and as the section attributes. Don't put secrets in `Environment=` lines; use `EnvironmentFile=` or credentials instead.
- The unit file is written atomically, but the provider does not lock `/etc/systemd/system` against other tools. A symlink at the unit's path, such as a unit masked with `systemctl mask`, is refused.
